      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249580/assets/296883eddf8dc4521e4fa1313e1f3176c965a376d55ecb9189fa913adc8d2625.png)\n\nPart of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $4$ is shown above. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-6\\le x\\le 6?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph above shows the function in the interval $-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\nSince this interval has length $4,$ and the period of the function is also $4,$ this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249580/assets/13eb143d92cd215f9283a44ad67ca91e679610d4b6625ae53ced56295430e843.png)",
